Keeps Chrome's warning before http out of an agent's tab, and has its text say the app's name
for the browser (//browser_core/hooks.h, //browser_core/secure_connections.h). The warning is a
dialog whose Continue to site loads the page over http and remembers the site: the user's to
press.

https_only_mode_tab_helper.cc: where Chrome would show the warning, the tab helper asks the core
first, handing it the navigation that was stopped; the core answers for the tab the page is in
and, in an agent's, puts the tab back itself, so no warning ever exists there. The helper holds
no rule of its own.

ask_before_http_dialog_controller.cc: the two paragraphs that name the browser take the name the
core hands them, the app's, or else the name the text had; and the Learn more link, which opens a
help page of Chrome's that isn't there, is gone, its place in the text left empty, in every
language.

security_interstitials_strings.grdp: those two paragraphs get a placeholder where they said
Chrome, and the one for a private window says "a private window" where it said Incognito mode.
Nothing else of the text changes. A changed message has no translation, so these three read in
English in every language while the rest of the dialog stays translated; a new major that rewords
one fails this hunk, loudly.

